e by 14.7--15.0% and increasing successful task completion on ColBench by up to 36.7%, showing that co-evolving the training system with the policy can substantially improve multi-turn RL.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2610.16523] EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents (https://borowiecki.dev/abs/2610.16523)\nciteturn120search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 days ago; Crawled: 2 days ago; View a PDF of the paper titled EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents, by Min Cai and Dan Zhang and Gao Xing and Libin Ma and Zhaohui Xu and Xi Ye ... We instead introduce EAU, a meta-agent framework for environment--agent--user co-evolution.\n\n# Computer Science > Artificial Intelligence\n\n[Submitted on 12 Oct 2026]\n\n# Title:EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents\n\nAuthors:Min Cai, Dan Zhang, Gao Xing, Libin Ma, Zhaohui Xu, Xi Ye\n\nView a PDF of the paper titled EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents, by Min Cai and Dan Zhang and Gao Xing and Libin Ma and Zhaohui Xu and Xi Ye\n\nView PDF HTML (experimental)\n\n> Abstract:Training LLM agents to interact with users over multiple turns is challenging because the learning signal depends not only on the agent, but also on the user simulator and environment, which jointly shape the agent's experience. Simulated users can be stochastic or inconsistent, while environments may contain unreliable tools, incomplete state coverage, and incorrect verifiers. Prior work mainly addresses the resulting instability through reward shaping or denser supervision, while treating these components as fixed. We instead introduce EAU, a meta-agent framework for environment--agent--user co-evolution. During training, EAU monitors trajectories, tool traces, rewards, and training statistics; diagnoses failures and uninformative interactions; and proposes targeted modifications to the user simulator, environment, reward design, and RL configuration. Validated changes are hot-swapped into the ongoing run, allowing the training setup to evolve together with the policy. These adaptations also persist across runs, so later training can build on previously discovered improvements rather than restarting from a fixed setup. We enable this process through an editable symbolic representation of user and environment dynamics, while the agent remains a neural LLM optimized with RL. Across three interactive-agent benchmarks and two backbone models, EAU consistently outperforms prior RL methods, improving the overall score over the strongest baseline by 14.7--15.0% and increasing successful task completion on ColBench by up to 36.7%, showing that co-evolving the training system with the policy can substantially improve multi-turn RL.\n\nSubjects:  | Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computation and Language (cs.CL); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nCite as:  | arXiv:2610.16523 [cs.AI]\n   | (or arXiv:2610.16523v1 [cs.AI] for this version)\n   | https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2610.16523 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite (pending registration)\n\n## Submission history\n\nFrom: Min Cai [view email]\n[v1] Mon, 12 Oct 2026 10:11:38 UTC (1,774 KB)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMuse: Meta's personal AI agent, features & capabilities (https://ai.meta.com/muse/)\nciteturn120search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; # Your personal AI agent that gets things done ... Eligible purchases are covered by Link’s purchase protections, a first for AI agents, and your conversations are not shared with Meta's ad systems. ... Muse is built with automated protections, agentic AI security standards, and user controls designed to prevent harmful content and unauthorized actions.\n\n## Introducing Muse\n\n# Your personal AI agent that gets things done\n\nImage\n\n## Introducing Muse\n\n# Your personal AI agent that gets things done\n\n## Introducing Muse\n\n# Your personal AI agent that gets things done\n\nScroll to explore\n\n## Take the busywork off your plate\n\nMuse gets to know you and your goals, working across all aspects of life, so you can focus on the things that matter. It comes with strong privacy and security built in, so you stay in control.\n\n\n### A secure computer with its own browser\n\nMuse Secure VM runs on a persistent, dedicated virtual machine equipped with a state-of-the-art browser to navigate the web. It books appointments, fills out forms and handles customer service.\n\nMuse Secure VM runs on a persistent, dedicated virtual machine equipped with a state-of-the-art browser to navigate the web.\nIt books appointments, fills out forms and handles customer service.\n\n\n### Designed to feel like a conversation\n\nChat with your Muse in the same way you message with other people, using the Muse app or directly in WhatsApp. \nA chatbot responds to one question at a time. An AI assistant can handle more complex conversations and generate content. A personal AI agent like Muse goes further: it can take real actions across the web and connected apps, work through multi-step tasks on its own, and keep running in the background even after you close the app.\n\n* * *\n\n### What can a personal AI agent help with?\n\nA personal AI agent can help with everyday tasks like booking reservations, monitoring prices, managing reminders, creating documents, generating images, and researching topics. It can also connect to third-party apps and services to take actions on your behalf, like sending emails or completing online purchases. See more agentic AI examples.\n\n\n## Explore Meta AI products\n\nImage: Meta AI personal assistant on mobile device\n\n### Meta AI Assistant\n\n#### Get more done with Meta AI, your personal AI assistant.\n\nTry Meta AI\n\nLearn more\n\nImage: Meta AI media generation output cards\n\n### Image Generation\n\n#### Explore fun presets, make precise edits and visualize your ideas.\n\nStart generating--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents · Pith (https://pith.science/paper/2610.16523)\nciteturn120search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 days ago; Crawled: yesterday; \"EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents.\" arXiv:2610.16523 (2026). https://pith.science/paper/EBSLUJDR ... The paper shows that co-evolving the user simulator, environment, and training configuration alongside the policy, mediated by a meta-agent, leads to substantial performance gains in multi-turn RL for user-centric agents.\n\n# EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents\n\nPaperPDF from arXiv\n\ndata files\n\nReviewThe objections and verdict, as JSON Paper breakdownThe authors' claims, conditions and measurements, as JSON Signed receiptProof this review has not been altered\n\nMin Cai, Dan Zhang, Gao Xing, Libin Ma, Zhaohui Xu, Xi Ye\n\nArtificial Intelligence · Submitted 12 Oct 2026 · 24 references\n\nPith's verdict: EAU proposes a meta-agent framework for co-evolving training components in interactive RL, with impressive empirical gains — but the comparison lacks matched compute budgets, making the headline improvements hard to attribute.\n\nT0 · AI review ▾\n\nHow this review was made: An AI referee (deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash) read the paper on October 14, 2026 and answered a fixed list of questions about it. T0 is the starting level every paper gets. The levels describe how deeply Pith has checked the paper, never the authors.\n### The paper claims\n\nCo-evolving the training system with the policy substantially improves multi-turn RL for user-centric agents.\n\n### The reading\n\nTraining large language model agents to interact with users over multiple turns is hard because the learning signal depends on the user simulator and environment, which are usually fixed and can be noisy, inconsistent, or uninformative. The paper argues that instead of only improving the policy's reward signal, one should also adapt the simulator, environment, and training configuration during training. It introduces EAU, a meta-agent framework that monitors ongoing RL training, diagnoses failures, and proposes targeted modifications to these components, which are then hot-swapped into the live run. Across three benchmarks and two backbone models, EAU consistently outperforms prior RL methods, improving overall scores by 14.7–15.0% and increasing successful task completion on ColBench by up to 36.7%.\n\n### What carries the argument\n\nEAU (Environment-Agent-User Co-Evolution) — a meta-agent framework where a frontier coding agent acts as a meta-controller that continuously monitors RL training, diagnoses issues in the user simulator, environment, and training dynamics, and proposes programmatic modifications via editable symbolic interfaces. The symbolic user simulator provides deterministic control over information disclosure, mixed with an LLM simulator via an epsilon-greedy strategy. Interventions occur both intra-run (hot-swapped without restart) and inter-run (carried across training runs).\n\nExtended reading notes\n\n### Core claim\n\nThe paper shows that co-evolving the user simulator, environment, and training configuration alongside the policy, mediated by a meta-agent, leads to substantial performance gains in multi-turn RL for user-centric agents. The meta-agent observes training trajectories, tool traces, rewards, and statistics; proposes modifications to the user simulator (including a symbolic user with tunable rules), reward design, episode limits, and RL hyperparameters; and validates and hot-swaps these changes into the ongoing training loop. This enables the training system itself to adapt as the policy improves, overcoming instabilities from fixed simulators and environments.\n\n### Load-bearing premise\n\nThe meta-agent (a frontier coding model) can reliably diagnose training problems from logs and propose beneficial modifications, even though the integrity gates only constrain writes and do not guarantee improvement.\n\nEditorial extensions\n\n### If this is right\n\n  * If EAU is sound, the standard practice of fixing the simulator and environment during interactive-agent RL training should be replaced by adaptive co-evolution.\n  * Meta-agent-guided adaptation could reduce the manual effort of debugging simulators, reward functions, and hyperparameters for multi-turn agent tasks.\n  * The observed gains (up to 36.7% on ColBench) suggest that training-system adaptation may be as important as policy optimization in achieving high performance.\n  * The method is compatible with multiple RL algorithms (PPO, UserRL, InfoPO) and meta-agent backbones (Claude Opus, Codex), indicating broad applicability.\n  * Performance improvements transfer to held-out environments (e.g., Inten. in UserGym), suggesting that co-evolution fosters generalizable agent behaviors.\n\nSimulated users can be stochastic or inconsistent, while environments may contain unreliable tools, incomplete state coverage, and incorrect verifiers. Prior work mainly addresses the resulting instability through reward shaping or denser supervision, while treating these components as fixed. We instead introduce EAU, a meta-agent framework for environment--agent--user co-evolution. During training, EAU monitors trajectories, tool traces, rewards, and training statistics; diagnoses failures and uninformative interactions; and proposes targeted modifications to the user simulator, environment, reward design, and RL configuration. Validated changes are hot-swapped into the ongoing run, allowing the training setup to evolve together with the policy. These adaptations also persist across runs, so later training can build on previously discovered improvements rather than restarting from a fixed setup. We enable this process through an editable symbolic representation of user and environment dynamics, while the agent remains a neural LLM optimized with RL. Across three interactive-agent benchmarks and two backbone models, EAU consistently outperforms prior RL methods, improving the overall score over the strongest baseline by 14.7--15.0% and increasing successful task completion on ColBench by up to 36.7%, showing that co-evolving the training system with the policy can substantially improve multi-turn RL.\n\nFigures\n\nImage: Figure 1 Figure 1. Illustration of how our meta-agent, EAU, manages RL training runs. EAU surpasses the prior SOTA on ColBench. Left: For the first three runs, EAU proactively restarts when it identifies issues that can only be resolved with offline patches. Middle: Run 4 and 5 displayed in blue and red are two full runs. Dashed lines indicate online, intra-run modifications. Run 4 explored individual modifications through local perturbations, whereas Run 5 used endpoint interventions followed by replication, reversal, and interaction tests. 1 arXiv:2610.16523v1 [cs.AI] 12 Oct 2026 Image: Figure 2 Figure 2. Illustration of a user￾centric agentic task. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBe There for Every Customer With Meta Business Agent (https://about.fb.com/news/2026/06/meta-business-agent?trk=article-ssr-frontend-pulse_x-social-details_comments-action_comment-text)\nciteturn120search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: 4 days ago;   * We’re introducing Meta Business Agent, which lets businesses of all sizes increase their output and deliver personalized experiences for customers using AI. ...   * We’re also introducing the Meta Business Agent Platform, which gives businesses the infrastructure to build, customize, and deploy their Business Agents at scale.\n\nBack to Newsroom\n\nMeta\n\n# Be There for Every Customer With Meta Business Agent\n\nJune 3, 2026June 3, 2026\n\nListen to Article 10 Pause 10\n\n[Select]\n\nImage\n\n## Takeaways\n\n  * We’re introducing Meta Business Agent, which lets businesses of all sizes increase their output and deliver personalized experiences for customers using AI.\n  * Business Agent also doubles as a partner to deliver a morning briefing to catch you up on chats you missed overnight and provide insights on your threads.\n  * We’re also introducing the Meta Business Agent Platform, which gives businesses the infrastructure to build, customize, and deploy their Business Agents at scale.\n\nToday, we’re introducing Meta Business Agent – AI that lets every business show up for every customer as if they had an infinite team behind them. Business Agent can be set up in minutes or plugged directly into your existing enterprise infrastructure so you can 10X or 100X output.\n\n \n\nMore than one million businesses are already using a Meta Business Agent on WhatsApp and Messenger to respond to customers around the clock. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nmeta-agent/README.md at main · canvas-org/meta-agent · GitHub (https://github.com/canvas-org/meta-agent/blob/main/README.md)\nciteturn120search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago;             messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": str(ctx.task)}], ... For detailed experiments, see Meta-Agent: Continual Learning for Agents. ...     @software{sleiman_meta_agent_2026,\n\n# Meta-Agent\n\nContinual learning for agents, without weight updates.\n\nMeta-Agent turns execution traces into persistent improvements to the system around a language model. It learns at two timescales:\n\n  * a fast loop improves the task-facing harness, including prompts, tools, hooks, stopping criteria, subagents, and control flow;\n  * an experimental slow loop improves the proposer skill that diagnoses failures and generates future harness changes.\n\nThe base model remains frozen. Meta-Agent keeps what it learns in harness code and versioned proposer instructions, backed by an on-disk experience store.\n\n> Research question: Can an agent improve both its harness and the process used to improve that harness?\n\nRead the technical report →\n\nMethod · Result · Quickstart\n\n## Overview\n\nAn agent is more than its model. The harness decides what context the model sees, which tools it can call, how it tracks state, when it checks its output, and when it stops.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents | Arxiv - DeepPaper (https://jp.ibbac.eu.org/papers/2610.16523v1)\nciteturn120search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 days ago; Crawled: yesterday; EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents ... EAU is a meta-agent framework that enables the co-evolution of environment, user simulator, and agent policy to improve multi-turn RL training stability and performance.\n\n  1.   2. Paper List\n  3. EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents\n\n# EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents\n\nPDF DeepRead\n\n### Key Contributions\n\nIntroduces a meta-agent framework (EAU) that dynamically adapts environment, user simulator, and reward design during training, leading to significant performance gains in multi-turn agent tasks.\n\n### Abstract\n\nTraining LLM agents to interact with users over multiple turns is challenging because the learning signal depends not only on the agent, but also on the user simulator and environment, which jointly shape the agent's experience. Simulated users can be stochastic or inconsistent, while environments may contain unreliable tools, incomplete state coverage, and incorrect verifiers. Prior work mainly addresses the resulting instability through reward shaping or denser supervision, while treating these components as fixed. We instead introduce EAU, a meta-agent framework for environment--agent--user co-evolution. During training, EAU monitors trajectories, tool traces, rewards, and training statistics; diagnoses failures and uninformative interactions; and proposes targeted modifications to the user simulator, environment, reward design, and RL configuration. Validated changes are hot-swapped into the ongoing run, allowing the training setup to evolve together with the policy. These adaptations also persist across runs, so later training can build on previously discovered improvements rather than restarting from a fixed setup. We enable this process through an editable symbolic representation of user and environment dynamics, while the agent remains a neural LLM optimized with RL. Across three interactive-agent benchmarks and two backbone models, EAU consistently outperforms prior RL methods, improving the overall score over the strongest baseline by 14.7--15.0% and increasing successful task completion on ColBench by up to 36.7%, showing that co-evolving the training system with the policy can substantially improve multi-turn RL.\n\n### Summary\n\nEAU is a meta-agent framework that enables the co-evolution of environment, user simulator, and agent policy to improve multi-turn RL training stability and performance.\n\n### Subjects\n\ncs.AI cs.CL cs.LG\n\n### arXiv\n\nImage: arXiv 2610.16523v1\n\n### Paper Type\n\nframework Method\n\n### Domain\n\nReinforcement Learning LLM Agent\n\nAuthors\n\nMin Cai, Dan Zhang, Gao Xing, Libin Ma, Zhaohui Xu, Xi Ye\n\nSubmitted\n\nOct 12, 2026--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAgents API - Meta Agents Research Environments (https://facebookresearch.github.io/meta-agents-research-environments/api_reference/agents.html)\nciteturn120search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Agents in Meta Agents Research Environments are responsible for: ... The environment event reaction loop (handling user messages, notifications, and the overall agent lifecycle) is implemented in are/simulation/agents/default_agent/are_simulation_main.py.\n\n# Agents API¶\n\nAgents are the AI entities that interact with the Agents Research Environments to complete tasks. This section documents the core Agent API and related classes for creating and customizing agent behavior.\n\n## Overview¶\n\nAgents in Meta Agents Research Environments are responsible for:\n\n  * Task Execution: Understanding and completing assigned tasks\n\n  * Tool Usage: Interacting with apps through their exposed APIs\n\n  * Reasoning: Making decisions based on available information\n\n  * Learning: Adapting behavior based on feedback and results\n\nThe agent system is built around a ReAct (Reasoning + Acting) framework that allows agents to think, act, and observe in iterative cycles.\n\n## Creating Custom Agents¶\n\nAgents used on the CLI are built through are/simulation/agents/default_agent/agent_factory.py. The `BaseAgent` class describes the “inner loop” of the agent - the core reasoning and action execution logic. The environment event reaction loop (handling user messages, notifications, and the overall agent lifecycle) is implemented in are/simulation/agents/default_agent/are_simulation_main.py.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nmeta-agents-research-environments/docs/foundations/index.rst at main · facebookresearch/meta-agents-research-environments · GitHub (https://github.com/facebookresearch/meta-agents-research-environments/blob/main/docs/foundations/index.rst?plain=true)\nciteturn120search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Meta Agents Research Environments is designed to address these gaps by offering strong foundations and abstractions that are general enough to support a wide range of tasks and benchmarks. ... - **Apps**: Similar to a desktop or mobile phone, the environment can support a set of apps that users use in their daily life, which the agent can interact with. ... In ARE, everything is an event, meaning any action the user, the agent takes or even external events generated by the environment itself, are events.\n\n* **Tool Usage**: Interacting with apps through their exposed APIs\n\n* **Reasoning**: Making decisions based on available information and context\n\n* **Learning**: Adapting behavior based on feedback and results\n\n* **Communication**: Sending messages to users and logging progress\n\n.. admonition:: Getting Started with Agents\n\nFor most use cases, we recommend using the default agent by specifying the ``--agent default``\n\nparameter when running CLI commands. This provides:\n\n* Robust ReAct loop implementation\n\n* Comprehensive logging and error handling\n\n* Flexible tool integration\n\n* Customizable system prompts and behavior\n\nThe default agent will automatically handle the event-based interactions with the environment, logging all actions and observations for later analysis.\n\nFor more details about the default agent implementation, see the ``ARE_react_json_agent`` function in ``ARE/agents/default_agent/agent_factory.py``.\n\nYou can also learn more about customizing agents in :doc:`../api_reference/agents` and explore practical examples in the tutorials.\n\n.. dropdown:: :octicon:`infinity` Universes\n\n:animate: fade-in\n\n**Universes** represent distinct, fully-populated instantiations of the simulated environment, each reflecting a specific user's digital world.\n\nThey provide the realistic foundation from which scenarios emerge, containing comprehensive synthetic data that mirrors authentic usage patterns.\n\n**Core Concept**\n\nUniverses embody a data-first approach to scenario development:\n\n* **Rich Environmental Foundation**: Each universe contains realistic application content including message histories, email threads, calendar events, and contacts\n\n* **Persona-Driven**: Built around detailed user personas that ensure coherence across all applications and data\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Zenera Platform | Zenera (https://zenera.ai/platform)\nciteturn120search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; At the center of Zenera sits the Meta-Agent - the user's single point of interaction for designing, building, and evolving agentic systems. ... The Meta-Agent decomposes the problem into specialized agents, defines their roles, selects appropriate models, and wires the entire system:\n\nPlatform\n\n# The Zenera Platform\n\nAn AI that builds AI systems - and proves they work before they run.\n\nTable of contents\n\n  * The Meta-Agent\n  * From Problem to Production\n  * Always-On: The Event-Reactive System\n  * Why Multi-Agent Systems Need a Meta-Agent\n  * Failure Modes of Human-Designed Agent Systems\n  * Why These Problems Scale Exponentially\n  * The Meta-Agent as Compiler, Linter, and Profiler\n  * Modification Safety: Change Without Fear\n  * Agent Certification\n  * Key Properties of Generated Systems\n\n## The Meta-Agent\n\nAt the center of Zenera sits the Meta-Agent - the user's single point of interaction for designing, building, and evolving agentic systems.\n\nThe Meta-Agent is not a chatbot. It is not a code generator. It is an AI system architect that translates business requirements into complete, verified, production-ready multi-agent systems.\n\n## From Problem to Production\n\n1\n\n### Describe the Problem\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nApps - Meta Agents Research Environments (https://facebookresearch.github.io/meta-agents-research-environments/foundations/apps.html)\nciteturn120search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; A first env we introduce primarily aims at training agents capable of evolving in environments similar to those of operating systems such as iOS, Android, Meta RayBan OS, QuestOS, etc. ... The Agent-User Interface (AUI) app is a required component in every environment, serving as the communication bridge between users and AI agents.\n\nNon-exhaustive list of apps native to the Agents Research Environments\n\n  * Contacts\n\n  * EmailClient\n\n  * Messaging\n\n  * FileSystem\n\n  * Calendar\n\n  * Shopping\n\n  * Cab\n\n  * City\n\n  * ApartmentListing\n\nAmong these apps, a couple play a crucial role in the execution of the environment.\n\nSome apps in the initial environment play a crucial role in the execution of scenarios. We give more details below about the most important ones, giving a glimpse of the possibilities offered by the platform. Thanks to the high flexibility of the system, it is also possible to integrate MCP interfaces ( MCPApp - Model Context Protocol) and any other interface as long as they are wrapped to match the platform’s standards. For instance, some recent efforts have integrated SQL databases to test agents’ compatibility with different search strategies.\n\nAgent-User Interface\n\nThe Agent-User Interface (AUI) app is a required component in every environment, serving as the communication bridge between users and AI agents. Unlike traditional chat models where interaction happens through direct messaging, our system models user-agent communication through a dedicated ARE application.\n\nThis design requires the agent to make specific tool calls when reporting information to the user, rather than simply generating responses. When users send messages to the agent through the AUI, their content is automatically injected into the agent’s memory for processing. We highlight that this is the current implementation choice and that better alternatives may exist, like using the Notification System to surface user messages to the agent (see Notifications).\n\nThis tool-based communication model provides several advantages:\n\n  * It allows precise control over when the agent should communicate with the user versus when it should continue working silently on tasks\n\n  * It enables users to interact with the agent even while the agent is actively engaged in completing other objectives\n\nThe AUI thus creates a more realistic interaction pattern that mirrors how people actually work with digital assistants in complex, multi-tasking environments.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents | The Latest in AI (https://thelatestinai.com/papers/2610.16523)\nciteturn120search10 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 days ago; Crawled: yesterday; # EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents ... We instead introduce EAU, a meta-agent framework for environment--agent--user co-evolution.\n\ncs.AI·2610.16523 Copy arXiv ID·Oct 12, 2026\n\nSave\n\n# EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents\n\nAuthors: Min Cai, Dan Zhang, Gao Xing, Libin Ma, Zhaohui Xu, Xi Ye\n\nOrganizations: University of Alberta · National University of Singapore · Kuaishou Technology · Independent Researcher · Alberta Machine Intelligence Institute (Amii)\n\n## Abstract\n\nTraining LLM agents to interact with users over multiple turns is challenging because the learning signal depends not only on the agent, but also on the user simulator and environment, which jointly shape the agent's experience. Simulated users can be stochastic or inconsistent, while environments may contain unreliable tools, incomplete state coverage, and incorrect verifiers. Prior work mainly addresses the resulting instability through reward shaping or denser supervision, while treating these components as fixed. We instead introduce EAU, a meta-agent framework for environment--agent--user co-evolution. During training, EAU monitors trajectories, tool traces, rewards, and training statistics; diagnoses failures and uninformative interactions; and proposes targeted modifications to the user simulator, environment, reward design, and RL configuration. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLaunching Meta Enterprise Platform (https